import{_ as t,o as s,c as n,a2 as o}from"./chunks/framework.BUOFcz_J.js";const p=JSON.parse('{"title":"Continuing any Agent session in Rho","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"records/session-continuity-episode.md","filePath":"records/session-continuity-episode.md"}'),a={name:"records/session-continuity-episode.md"};function i(d,e,r,c,h,l){return s(),n("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[o('<div class="warning custom-block"><p class="custom-block-title">Historical record</p><p>This page records what happened on a date, with its observed failures and limits. It is not a requirement for the current implementation. Current behaviour is in <a href="/Rho/docs/reference/status">Status</a>.</p></div><h1 id="continuing-any-agent-session-in-rho" tabindex="-1">Continuing any Agent session in Rho <a class="header-anchor" href="#continuing-any-agent-session-in-rho" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Continuing any Agent session in Rho&quot;">​</a></h1><p>Date: 2026-11-07. Source changes after Rho 0.5.5, with Agent 0.4.30 in Rho-plugins. Core is unchanged. Package publication and installation are separate.</p><h2 id="question" tabindex="-1">Question <a class="header-anchor" href="#question" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Question&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The person asked why a conversation started outside Rho was read-only in Rho: with the same Agent, a session should continue seamlessly wherever it started. They also found the Agent settings panel ugly, and the two kinds of conversation and the ways of connecting an Agent confusing. They pointed to <a href="https://github.com/yetone/magpie" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">magpie</a> for how it manages Agents and sessions.</p><p>The read-only rule had been a product decision (2026-10-25: whoever starts a session holds its input), not a limit of the Agents. Rho already continued its own conversations by loading the Agent&#39;s session over ACP, and the bundled adapters (claude-agent-acp 0.97.0, codex-acp 2.3.0) list, load, resume and fork sessions. Harness&#39;s <code>dsh acp</code> profile resumes sessions too.</p><h2 id="what-magpie-does-and-what-rho-takes-from-it" tabindex="-1">What magpie does, and what Rho takes from it <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-magpie-does-and-what-rho-takes-from-it" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What magpie does, and what Rho takes from it&quot;">​</a></h2><p>magpie is a gateway and a reader of each Agent&#39;s own session files. It treats each Agent&#39;s own record and session ID as the truth and keeps only a derived cache. It lists sessions started anywhere, so nothing is imported, and builds the <code>claude --resume</code>/<code>codex resume</code> command on the server. Where Agents keep locks it defers to them, plus a one-minute recent-write check. It is not an Agent host and has no composer, so continuing a session there means a terminal.</p><p>Rho runs Agents, so it continues the session in place. It reads the same native records and adds no session identity of its own for them.</p><h2 id="what-changed" tabindex="-1">What changed <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-changed" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What changed&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>One list.</strong> Conversations shows every session in the project folder: Rho&#39;s conversations, Claude Code and Codex sessions read from their own files, and Harness conversations observed by the dsh-rho adapter. The &quot;Outside Rho&quot; type is gone. A row names its Agent and where it started (Terminal, VS Code, Codex app, Script…), and shows when another window has it open. Once Rho continues a session, its row is that conversation; the session is not listed twice.</li><li><strong>Continue anywhere.</strong> Opening a row shows the session&#39;s history in the Agent panel, and the next message continues that same session: <code>session/load</code> for Claude Code and Codex, <code>session/resume</code> for Harness. Turns sent later from a terminal appear in place between Rho&#39;s own, matched by their text. A row and the panel copy the terminal command (<code>cd … &amp;&amp; claude --resume ID</code>).</li><li><strong>Open elsewhere.</strong> Claude Code names each live process and the session it holds in <code>sessions/&lt;pid&gt;.json</code>; Codex keeps <code>thread-writer-locks/&lt;id&gt;.lock</code>. While another process holds the session, Rho refuses to write into it and says where it is open. Rho&#39;s own adapter&#39;s child processes do not count. The person can instead continue a copy (ACP <code>session/fork</code>); the original record is not written. Harness cannot fork; a Harness session is refused only while Harness is answering in it.</li><li><strong>Stale connections.</strong> An idle connection that Rho kept open does not know about turns sent from another window. When the session&#39;s record changed after Rho&#39;s last message, Rho closes that connection and loads the session again.</li><li><strong>Agents tab.</strong> It replaces &quot;New conversation settings&quot; and &quot;Connect your agent&quot;. It lists the Agents this service can start, with a radio button for new conversations, a model chip and folded settings; an Agent that is not found is shown greyed out. Below that is the setup prompt for using the project from an Agent window the person runs elsewhere. DeepSeek Harness is detected when <code>dsh</code> and its <code>acp</code> profile are installed, without starting anything.</li><li><strong>Session markers.</strong> Starting Claude Code from a Rho that was itself started inside Claude Code (as an Agent running the setup does) passed on <code>CLAUDE_CODE_CHILD_SESSION</code>. Claude Code then saves no transcript, which was observed directly (&quot;Transcript saving is off — inherited CLAUDE_CODE_CHILD_SESSION marker&quot;). The adapter now drops those markers.</li></ul><p><code>agent.sessions.list</code> and <code>agent.sessions.read</code> remain local-person only and excluded from Agent grants. <code>conversation.start</code> takes <code>continue: { session, copy? }</code>. Listing and reading open files only and start no Agent. Native reads are bounded: 64 sessions, 256 KiB head and tail for a listing, a streamed read of at most 256 MiB, 200 turns, 256 tools per turn.</p><h2 id="real-runs" tabindex="-1">Real runs <a class="header-anchor" href="#real-runs" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Real runs&quot;">​</a></h2><p><code>node scripts/session-continuity-episode.mjs</code> uses a disposable project, Rho state and Claude Code configuration (credentials only in the environment), a real Claude Code (haiku), Chrome, and an interactive Claude Code in a pseudo-terminal to hold a session open. The final run, after the last source changes (<code>work/session-continuity/1794039903484</code>), passed:</p><ol><li><code>claude -p</code> in the project folder started a session (&quot;remember heron-42&quot;). Rho listed it as started in Script, with no Rho conversation.</li><li>Continue opened it in the panel. The next message (&quot;What code word…?&quot;) ran in the same session ID and answered <code>heron-42</code>.</li><li><code>claude -p --resume</code> in a terminal answered what Rho had asked. The panel showed that turn in place.</li><li>With an interactive Claude Code holding the session, the API and the panel refused the message (&quot;This session is open in Terminal&quot;).</li><li>Released, Rho&#39;s next message asked for the previous message word for word. It quoted the terminal&#39;s prompt. In Claude Code&#39;s own record its parent is the terminal&#39;s answer, not Rho&#39;s earlier message.</li><li>A second session, held open, continued as a copy that answered <code>osprey-7</code>. The original transcript does not contain the copy&#39;s message.</li><li>Conversations and Agents had no overflow or unnamed buttons at 1440, 1024, 768 and 390 px, with the panel closed.</li></ol><p>Earlier runs found real defects:</p><ul><li>The first time window for matching Rho&#39;s turns took the terminal turn for Rho&#39;s; matching now uses the turn&#39;s text.</li><li>Treating every holder as Rho&#39;s while Rho&#39;s connection was open hid the interactive terminal; Rho now checks the holder&#39;s parent process.</li><li>A <code>.session-row</code> class collided with the panel&#39;s usage strip and cut the row short.</li><li>At 390 px the panel covered the page, so those screenshots measured the panel; the run now closes it first.</li><li>One answer was too vague to show that Rho saw the terminal turn; the check now asks for a quotation and reads the record&#39;s parent link.</li></ul><p>Harness was checked separately (<code>work/session-continuity/harness/resume.mjs</code>): <code>dsh headless</code> started a session (&quot;remember kestrel-9&quot;), Rho&#39;s ACP driver resumed it through <code>dsh acp</code> under the same session ID and answered <code>kestrel-9</code>, and <code>dsh headless --session-id</code> then answered what the driver had asked. A service started from the CLI reported Claude Code, Codex and DeepSeek Harness as available, with Claude Code still the default.</p><h2 id="checks" tabindex="-1">Checks <a class="header-anchor" href="#checks" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Checks&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><code>test/session-continuity.test.mjs</code> (new): native listing and reading from fixture Claude Code and Codex records, with nothing started; another folder&#39;s session excluded; a held session refused; a copy forked and the original never loaded; continuing in place loads that session ID; a second continue refused; a later holder refused on send; Codex continues with Codex.</li><li>53 related Rho tests pass with the debug Core selected: ACP conversations, Agent connection, observation, settings, browser, presentation, conversations, evidence, external sessions and continuity. 32 Agent plugin tests pass (ACP, external sessions and ACP observation, process, intents, describe, activity). <code>npm run check:contracts</code> is current.</li><li><code>node scripts/agent-page-acceptance.mjs</code> passes in Chrome after its steps moved to the Agents tab.</li><li>CI run 42356741141 for <code>81471c2c</code> failed one test on macOS, Linux and Windows: the underlined link read <code>--color-text</code>, which no stylesheet declares, so it silently kept the notice&#39;s grey. It now reads <code>--color-ink</code>. The full local suite then failed once in <code>acp-conversations.test.mjs</code>: that test took a finished turn as settled before the folder comparison was attached to it (an older race, seen under load). It now waits for the comparison. The full suite then passed: 315 tests, 2 skipped, none failed. CI run 42357593474 for <code>94d363d1</code> then passed every job: the four Cores, tests on macOS, Linux and Windows, the packed candidate and its offline installs.</li><li>The screens were read at each step. The Agents tab and held-session notice were shot again after the narrow-width and link-spacing fixes. In the final run, &quot;continue a copy&quot; read as plain text beside the notice; it is now underlined, checked again with a listed session held by a live process.</li></ul><h2 id="limits" tabindex="-1">Limits <a class="header-anchor" href="#limits" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Limits&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Codex continuation was checked with a test Agent and the person&#39;s real Codex records (read only), not a real Codex model run. Harness sessions are listed only through the dsh-rho adapter&#39;s observation; a <code>dsh headless</code> session is not listed, though it continues. Native Harness records were not read directly. Turns sent from another window are matched to Rho&#39;s by their words, so two identical messages close together can pair wrongly. An idle Claude Code window that holds a session counts as open, as Claude Code records it; a Codex lock file is taken as open without testing the lock. Other clients, Windows and independent users were not observed.</p><h2 id="npm-delivery" tabindex="-1">npm delivery <a class="header-anchor" href="#npm-delivery" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;npm delivery&quot;">​</a></h2><p>On 2026-11-07 the user selected publication of the latest combined sources. The remote <code>main</code> heads matched the clean checkouts: Core <code>8bed8ef5dbb841c61845317b330e2e7f755d8f50</code> and Plugins <code>434d9a33d82125e7b3f291fae83e7b33a0fc2a24</code>, already selected in <code>components.json</code>. Rho includes every commit since <code>v0.5.5</code>, through the retained-evidence and checkout cleanup at <code>26c8cb2f</code>. No component source change or local override is needed to assemble them.</p><p>The registry&#39;s current <code>latest</code> was 0.5.5. Version 0.5.6 selects these sources, the Agent connection prompt, session continuation and its fixes, and the retained sclet reports. <a href="https://github.com/YuLab-SMU/Rho/actions/runs/42360742470" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">CI 42360742470</a> passed every job on <code>26c8cb2f</code>, including the three test systems and four-platform offline installs with Linux Node 24 and 26. That candidate still names 0.5.5; the version change needed its own CI candidate before tagging <code>v0.5.6</code>.</p><p><a href="https://github.com/YuLab-SMU/Rho/actions/runs/42361803786" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">CI 42361803786</a> on release commit <code>cd559cc0f832cd4f68386caa351ddad942f41e21</code> passed all 15 jobs. macOS and Linux each passed 315 of 317 tests, with two skipped; Windows passed 300, with 17 skipped and no failures. The same candidate passed all six offline installation checks: macOS arm64, Linux x64/arm64, Windows x64, and Linux x64 on Node 24 and 26. Its 3,166-file tarball is 25,665,590 bytes, with SHA-256 <code>022694a50a6a0b437f3d013a25df5de7e3a8c43a6a6b4594d7e0fd1235ad0eaf</code>. The composition matches the selected sources above, with no local overrides.</p><p>Tag <code>v0.5.6</code> names that release commit. <a href="https://github.com/YuLab-SMU/Rho/actions/runs/42362238297" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Release 42362238297</a> found the candidate, verified its hash and installation reports, and published it without rebuilding. npm returned the publication receipt at 10:03:07 UTC on 2026-11-07, with <a href="https://search.sigstore.dev/?logIndex=3735209363" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">provenance log entry 3735209363</a>. The exact-version registry endpoint still returned 404 at 10:03:45 UTC. A local read first found 0.5.6 at 10:58:41 UTC, about 56 minutes after the receipt. The 25,665,590 downloaded bytes matched both the candidate SHA-256 above and npm&#39;s SHA-512 integrity. The registry now selects 0.5.6 as <code>latest</code>.</p><p>A fresh <code>npm install --omit=dev --ignore-scripts @rho-workspace/rho@0.5.6</code> into a disposable prefix succeeded. Its lock names the public registry tarball and the same integrity; the installed CLI&#39;s <code>--help</code> exits 0, its composition matches all three selected source trees with no overrides, and all four installed Core binaries match their recorded hashes and sizes. The macOS arm64 binary retains its executable permission. These are installation and integrity checks, not another real-model trial.</p><p>During that wait, the maintainer&#39;s <a href="https://www.npmjs.com/package/@rho-workspace/rho?activeTab=versions" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Versions page</a> showed 0.5.6 as <code>Validating</code>, explicitly identifying unfinished automated review. For the registry&#39;s scan timing, see the <a href="https://github.blog/changelog/2026-07-28-npm-publish-time-malware-scanning-and-dual-use-metadata/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">official scanning notice</a>. An inventory of this exact tarball found 86,586,989 unpacked bytes: 25,484,472 in the four Core binaries and 38,768,199 in bundled dependencies. These measurements do not identify which content, if any, caused this version&#39;s delay.</p><p>The website was assembled from checked source <code>515ec7b1</code> and published as <code>gh-pages</code> commit <code>da2822a9</code>. Only <code>index.html</code> and <code>docs/</code> changed; all 124 files outside them, including the recorded demo, update feeds, setup, Skills and market, kept their original bytes. The generated new record page had two whitespace-only lines normalized before committing. Local checks covered 4,488 links and assets and 806 anchors across 66 HTML pages. The updated home/version area, Status and this delivery record were inspected at 1440 and 390 pixels without page overflow or page errors.</p><p><a href="https://github.com/YuLab-SMU/Rho/actions/runs/42366857163" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Pages run 42366857163</a> first stayed in <code>syncing_files</code> until the action&#39;s ten-minute timeout. Its cancellation request was acknowledged; a later deployment read returned an empty status, retained as unknown. Rerunning only the failed deploy job reused the 8,913,779-byte built site artifact on the same commit and succeeded on attempt 2. The native deployment read then said <code>succeed</code>. The legacy latest-build endpoint still returned <code>errored</code> with its earlier 11:18:13 UTC timestamp; that read is kept separate from the retry and public delivery facts.</p><p>At 11:22:57 UTC, all 97 added or changed public files matched the selected website bytes, including the homepage and the updated Status and episode. Eight unchanged public files had already matched during the first read and were reused, giving 105 checked paths in the retained report. The public homepage names npm 0.5.6.</p><p>This is the existing npm/WebUI release route. No existing service is replaced and no signed desktop build is published. The independently delivered dsh-rho 0.1.5 and its pinned market entry do not change. The real-model coverage and native-client limits above remain; this publication does not add a new model run.</p>',33)])])}const m=t(a,[["render",i]]);export{p as __pageData,m as default};
